'''uncolored, uncoloured''' (un-kul′ọrd), ''p. a.'' '''1.''' Not colored; not stained or dyed; hence, unclouded; clear; specifically, white.
{{smaller block|Things ''uncoloured'' and transparent. {{right|''Bacon.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>To deck with clouds the ''uncolour’d'' sky,
Or wet the thirsty earth with falling showers.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., v. 189.}}}}
'''2.''' Not heightened in description; truthful; unbiased: as, an ''uncolored'' statement.—'''3.''' Unadorned; plain; chaste.
{{smaller block|The contrast was remarkable between the ''uncolored'' style of his [John Foster’s] general diction, and the brilliant felicity of occasional images embroidered upon the sober ground of his text.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Biog. and Hist. Essays, p. 350.}}}}

'''uncolt''' (un-kōlt′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''colt.''] To unhorse; deprive of a colt or horse. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Thou liest; thou art not colted, thou art ''uncolted.''
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 2. 42.}}}}

'''uncomber†''', ''v.'' See ''uncumber''.

'''uncombine''' (un-kom-bīn′), ''v.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''combine.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To sever or destroy the combination, union, or junction of; separate; disconnect.
{{smaller block|Outbreaking vengeance ''uncombines'' the ill-joined plots.
{{right|''Daniel'', Civil Wars, iii.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become separated or disconnected. [Rare in both uses.]
